00:00:10.095 --> 00:00:11.895
Yeah, so Rocket.

00:00:11.895 --> 00:00:18.960
So, Rocket is a web framework for Rust that ideally makes it simple to write secure,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.159
correct web applications without sacrificing flexibility,

00:00:23.159 --> 00:00:24.210
so you're able to do what you want,

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.940
not what the web framework wants you to do or performance.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.680
It should be really, really fast,

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.350
even if we have all these really, really nice guarantees.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.810
Just to be very clear, what I mean by web framework.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.989
I mean, these types of things like Django,

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:38.115
or Spring, or Rails, you know,

00:00:38.115 --> 00:00:41.570
libraries that help you write sort of web backend code,

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.480
your web application's backend.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.450
And if you're not familiar with Rust,

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.730
it is a programming language sort of,

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.140
mostly developed by Mozilla.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:54.065
Uh, started at Mozilla research that is memory safe without a garbage collector using,

00:00:54.065 --> 00:00:55.690
you know, PL stuff.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:56.970
It has no data races,

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:58.200
guarantee at compile time.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.355
If your thing compiles,

00:00:59.355 --> 00:01:01.995
then you don't have any data races.

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:03.630
It also has a very, very,

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:08.040
very small runtime like on the order of C++ or even smaller,

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.935
so you can write things like operating systems.

00:01:09.935 --> 00:01:15.605
And sort of most necessary for Rocket is that it has macro systems,

00:01:15.605 --> 00:01:17.855
let's say systems because it has two,

00:01:17.855 --> 00:01:23.175
not just one but two and sort of the C style declarative kind of macros,

00:01:23.175 --> 00:01:26.070
but there's also this different kind of macro called procedural

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:30.190
macro which effectively gives you an AST at compile time.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:33.720
It lets you execute arbitrary Rust code that

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:38.580
generates other Rust code that is then inserted into the user's program.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.920
So, this is what Rocket uses,

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.710
sort of most of Rocket's niceties and

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.650
security and correct these properties come from procedural macros.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:51.105
The fact that we're able to take a user's web application and change it,

00:01:51.105 --> 00:01:53.040
explore it, verify it, validate it,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.085
at compile time to give all these nice guarantees.

00:01:56.085 --> 00:01:59.670
So, yeah, Rocket is a web framework for Rust that makes it simple to write secure,

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:03.270
correct web applications without sacrificing flexibility or performance.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:07.410
Now, a big part of this talk will be sort of focusing on the properties,

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.440
the secure, the security and correct properties that Rocket gives you.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.780
But I want to emphasize is that the vast majority of

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.560
these properties are being guaranteed at compile time.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.960
That is if your program compiles,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.100
you have all of these guarantees about what it, what it can and cannot do.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.710
For instance, Rocket will guarantee at compile time that there

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.080
are no cross-site scripting or CSRF vulnerabilities.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.690
It will guarantee at compile time that you don't have any directory traversal attacks.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.745
It will guarantee at compile time that you have no SQL Injection attacks.

00:02:35.745 --> 00:02:38.250
If your thing compiles, you know all these things to be true.

