[NOISE] So, the fix is well let's check that the person, you know,

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:26.635
the entity executing this web request is in fact the administrator,

00:12:26.635 --> 00:12:28.110
and how how would you fix this?

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.800
Well, if you're WordPress then you're written in PHP,

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:34.490
and you insert all of these if statements to check that you know,

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:38.200
that user logged in has a certain permission that you require.

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:41.645
And here I show four but in fact there were like eight or 12,

00:12:41.645 --> 00:12:45.560
and there was a separate- separate sort of patch set to fix other issues,

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.455
but sort of the fix here is to add these if conditions.

00:12:48.455 --> 00:12:50.720
If you actually look at these WordPress files you'll see

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:53.690
a bunch of if conditions for things that look exactly like this.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:56.035
[NOISE] So, by contrast,

00:12:56.035 --> 00:12:57.415
how would we do this in Rocket.

00:12:57.415 --> 00:12:59.710
Well i n Rocket we sort of would do what we just showed in

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:02.210
the overview you'd have this parameter that says,

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:03.720
"Oh this is a user" you know,

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:05.305
so we had this before in the example.

00:13:05.305 --> 00:13:07.060
So, we have this parameter that says this a user,

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:09.270
in this case we don't need a regular user,

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:14.800
we need an admin, so we change that to be an admin there so we now require an admin.

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:18.935
And now, this function can only execute if there is an administrator logged in.

00:13:18.935 --> 00:13:23.025
So, by virtue of simply declaring that you need an administrator,

00:13:23.025 --> 00:13:27.350
then you know, you know that this can all execute if there is in fact an administrator.

00:13:27.350 --> 00:13:29.750
Now, you might say what if I forget to actually put

00:13:29.750 --> 00:13:33.035
this administrative parameter on there as sort of WordPress did,

00:13:33.035 --> 00:13:36.920
presumably they had no data dependency between the parameter, this you know,

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:40.210
this check, and uh, and um you know,

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:41.725
the fact that it exists.

00:13:41.725 --> 00:13:42.945
So, you know, yeah well,

00:13:42.945 --> 00:13:44.845
the nice thing is that we cover that, too.

00:13:44.845 --> 00:13:46.070
So, if you look at this function body,

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:49.080
you'll see that we have this file: open_sensitive method.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:53.015
And the open_sensitive will require a value of type admin.

00:13:53.015 --> 00:13:54.995
In this case, you know, this user type.

00:13:54.995 --> 00:13:58.550
And so, what we've created here is a sort of capability-based data dependency.

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:01.110
i.e., we required admin to exist,

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:04.665
thus if the admin exists then we can call this open_sensitive method.

00:14:04.665 --> 00:14:06.280
If this admin does not exist,

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:08.150
we can't call this open sensitive method.

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:10.960
Thus, this open sensitive method can only be called

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:13.880
if an administrator was in fact authenticated by the request.

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:17.630
And so, this vulnerability that happened at WordPress is just caught at compile time.

00:14:17.630 --> 00:14:20.605
If you can't say that there is an administrator authenticated,

00:14:20.605 --> 00:14:22.220
you can't call this sensitive method.

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:23.710
[NOISE] Okay.

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:25.310
So, that's the WordPress vulnerability.

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:28.860
Next we want to talk about is Spree Commerce which is a sort

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:32.410
of shopping cart application written in Rails,

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:35.630
it's what Chipotle uses to run their online store.

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:39.385
[NOISE] Um, so I- I don't have a CVE for this,

00:14:39.385 --> 00:14:40.740
the- they I don't know what happened to it.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:42.235
They got rid of it it was weird.

00:14:42.235 --> 00:14:43.310
It existed and it didn't.

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:44.830
I don't know what happened.

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:47.155
Um, so the vulnerability goes like this,

00:14:47.155 --> 00:14:50.520
"By passing the right parameters while updating a user,

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:53.420
that user is able to assign any existing role to themselves."

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:55.530
That means if you're Bob,

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:59.240
and you're just you know, shopping for tacos on Chipotle's website,

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:01.195
and you really want to be the Chipotle administrator,

00:15:01.195 --> 00:15:02.220
then you just send

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:05.210
a particularly crafted web request and you become the Chipotle administrator.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.590
[NOISE] The vulnerability looked like this;

00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:11.140
they had some code that updated the user,

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:14.345
and this isn't Ruby so everything's sort of dynamically typed,

00:15:14.345 --> 00:15:16.400
and there was this params hash that just

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:18.450
gets the parameters for the incoming web request.

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:22.325
So, if you were to index it with this colon user the symbol,

00:15:22.325 --> 00:15:25.675
then you would get all of the all of the properties of the user passed in,

00:15:25.675 --> 00:15:26.950
and then what the function did,

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:31.655
what the method did was update to the current user to have those parameters.

00:15:31.655 --> 00:15:34.595
So, this is some magical Ruby dynamic stuff happening here

00:15:34.595 --> 00:15:38.230
which would be really difficult to do in a language like Rust which is statically typed.

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:42.590
Um, but sort of what goes wrong here is that the user passes in

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:48.415
a property of role as a parameter then you just update the role in the user.

00:15:48.415 --> 00:15:52.570
Sort of it just takes whatever the user passed in and saves that to the database.

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:55.100
That's sort of, that's the other thing going on here in Rails,

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:57.840
everything is sort of tied to the database so if you update

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:00.860
your user object it updates the user object in the database.

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:05.450
[NOISE] Now, uh, this is this is sort of one instance of

00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:08.335
a really big class of vulnerabilities that kind of impacted

00:16:08.335 --> 00:16:12.140
the entire Rails world because this is the canonical way to write a Rails application.

00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:14.960
You sort of do this mass assignment thing, and everyone was like,

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:17.335
"This is awesome that we can do this and Ruby and Rails",

00:16:17.335 --> 00:16:19.610
until they realize oh the whole thing is actually

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:22.270
a vulnerability which was termed a mass assignment vulnerability.

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:24.265
So, a bunch of web applications sort of had to

00:16:24.265 --> 00:16:26.810
stop doing the canonical thing to fix themselves.

00:16:26.810 --> 00:16:29.475
[NOISE] Now, how would it how would this look like in Rocket.

00:16:29.475 --> 00:16:31.460
Well in Rocket if you wanted to take parameters from

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:35.385
the outside world while as with everything else in Rocket they have to be typed.

00:16:35.385 --> 00:16:37.680
So, if you wanted, oh sorry, this is the fix.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:39.460
This is what they did to fix it.

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:42.530
They just deleted the role from the incoming parameters.

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:47.100
[NOISE] So, that was pretty funny because well, you gotta see the issue.

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:50.830
Okay. Yes. So, in Rocket if you wanted to actually do something like this,

00:16:50.830 --> 00:16:54.109
well you'd have to type the incoming parameters,

00:16:54.109 --> 00:16:58.120
so it looked kind of like this, we have some structure call in a user,

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:00.900
and then you'd specify the field so say it had a name,

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:02.790
and age, and you know, whatever other things.

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:05.330
[NOISE] And then you declare your route over here,

00:17:05.330 --> 00:17:07.165
and you would say I'm expecting some data,

00:17:07.165 --> 00:17:09.940
and the data is gonna have the parameter name user,

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:14.795
and the type for that is going to be a form that is going to be serialized into a user.

00:17:14.795 --> 00:17:17.390
All right, what Rocket will do is that will generate all the code for

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:19.570
you so this derive from form on top of

00:17:19.570 --> 00:17:24.835
this user struct generates the deserialization code for a for a form wwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwwcoming in as a user,

00:17:24.835 --> 00:17:28.730
and then you know, on line eight here that declares this user type to be of

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:31.020
that form type which is what actually is going to

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:33.820
execute the deserialization automatically for you.

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:38.250
So, there's no pop- if they pass in a role field deserialization will fail.

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:41.340
You can also ask it to succeed but in any case you're not going to keep that data.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:43.310
So, there's no way for you to ever

00:17:43.310 --> 00:17:46.350
possibly save a role in the database if you do it this way.

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:47.410
That would be really, really weird.

00:17:47.410 --> 00:17:48.825
[NOISE] Okay.

00:17:48.825 --> 00:17:50.195
So, that's Spree Commerce.

