00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:16.170
variable- this age parameter

00:24:16.170 --> 00:24:19.330
but you didn't say- have anything in the function declaration.

00:24:19.330 --> 00:24:21.440
Here- here's what your function declaration actually looks like.

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:24.930
So, go fix it." So we fix that.

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:27.735
Um, you know, then everything works okay.

00:24:27.735 --> 00:24:30.210
Every parameter is also associated with a type,

00:24:30.210 --> 00:24:32.000
this is just Rust.

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:35.325
Uh, and in particular in Rocket,

00:24:35.325 --> 00:24:38.395
every type has to implement a certain interface,

00:24:38.395 --> 00:24:40.550
in Rust, we call them traits.

00:24:40.550 --> 00:24:43.710
But you can think about them as Java interfaces or go

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:47.530
interfaces or interfaces basically everywhere if you have Haskell type classes.

00:24:47.530 --> 00:24:53.270
Um, and any type that implements the FromParam interface is allowed in that position.

00:24:53.270 --> 00:24:54.520
That includes your own types.

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:57.420
So, here, Rocket has implemented them for the sort of

00:24:57.420 --> 00:25:00.875
standard library type string and u8 which is an eight-bit integer.

00:25:00.875 --> 00:25:02.260
But, you can have your own types,

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:04.825
we saw like user and paste ID before.

00:25:04.825 --> 00:25:07.680
So, you can implement them for whatever you want.

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:09.690
And then, sort of when this executes,

00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:14.450
when this- when- when this- when there is a request that matches this- these parameters,

00:25:14.450 --> 00:25:17.600
this get/name/age, then Rocket will execute

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:22.225
the FromParam implementation which actually does the validation and conversion.

00:25:22.225 --> 00:25:24.400
So, in this case, for the age parameter it'll actually

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:26.565
check if it can parse it as an eight-bit integer.

00:25:26.565 --> 00:25:28.605
If it can, it'll parse it as an eight-bit integer,

00:25:28.605 --> 00:25:30.810
and then pass it in as the age parameter.

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:33.600
And so this is how we get sort of- in this particular case

00:25:33.600 --> 00:25:37.570
a tiny form of input validation for segments and parameters.

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:40.180
So, one place where this is kind of fun, uh,

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:42.030
sort of what this is the- totally applicable

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:44.180
is in preventing directory traversal attacks.

00:25:44.180 --> 00:25:50.145
So, if we have this code here which is going to fetch a- a file from our file system.

00:25:50.145 --> 00:25:54.335
So, here that gets the- the attribute says that we want

00:25:54.335 --> 00:25:56.430
any path the dot dot says not only

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:58.950
give me this segment but every other segment thereafter.

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:03.005
And the type of that thing is PathBuf which is a heap allocated path.

00:26:03.005 --> 00:26:06.900
And Rocket's implementation for FromParam is actually going to

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:11.550
verify that the path is safe to be used as a suffix to any other path.

00:26:11.550 --> 00:26:14.130
In particular, uh, sort of what Dan was alluding to before,

00:26:14.130 --> 00:26:16.120
it will ensure that there are no dot dots in that path.

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:20.195
It'll do, uh, it'll- it'll make that path canonical so you can use it as a suffix.

00:26:20.195 --> 00:26:21.880
And so, that means that we can write

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:25.035
a static file server literally in four lines like this.

00:26:25.035 --> 00:26:28.405
We just take the path, and then we prepend it with another path,

00:26:28.405 --> 00:26:29.895
and we return that,

00:26:29.895 --> 00:26:31.280
if it exists. And that's it.

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:33.910
So this is how you write a sort of four-line, uh,

00:26:33.910 --> 00:26:36.170
static file server in Rocket that

00:26:36.170 --> 00:26:39.285
is immune to directory traversal attacks. Which is pretty fun.

00:26:39.285 --> 00:26:41.835
Okay. So that's pretty, all I want to say about.

00:26:41.835 --> 00:26:43.760
There- it's not too difficult to sort of do this.

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:45.120
The name parameter thing, uh,

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:47.190
where it gets much more interesting in sort of trying to be

00:26:47.190 --> 00:26:49.890
arbitrary about the input validation that you're trying to do.

00:26:49.890 --> 00:26:52.170
And the mechanism that Rocket has for this,

00:26:52.170 --> 00:26:54.000
uh, we call Request Guards.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:55.280
So, Request Guards I think are

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:59.270
the most interesting part about Rocket that sort of- I haven't seen any other web programming,

00:26:59.270 --> 00:27:01.710
that does anything like Request Guards, which is really fun.

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:03.470
So, what are Requests Guards?

00:27:03.470 --> 00:27:06.840
So, Request Guards are these parameters that

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:11.355
appear in your route that don't have a matching parameter in the attribute.

00:27:11.355 --> 00:27:13.805
So, we here we have get/admin.

00:27:13.805 --> 00:27:15.980
A- admin is not in brackets,

00:27:15.980 --> 00:27:18.580
thus it's not sort of one of these dynamic parameters.

00:27:18.580 --> 00:27:22.665
And, but we still have this admin parameter inside of the function.

00:27:22.665 --> 00:27:25.405
And because it doesn't have a matching parameter in the attribute,

00:27:25.405 --> 00:27:26.660
it's called a Request Guard.

00:27:26.660 --> 00:27:28.905
[NOISE] And you can have as many of these as you want,

00:27:28.905 --> 00:27:30.565
here we just have one.

00:27:30.565 --> 00:27:32.360
We can have as many of these as you want.

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:36.990
And the restriction on these types is that they have to implement a different trait,

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:40.815
a different type class, a different interface called FromRequest.

00:27:40.815 --> 00:27:42.400
And it can do whatever it wants.

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:44.370
Just like, we saw before we had a user,

00:27:44.370 --> 00:27:45.520
and then we had this admin type.

00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:47.400
Here, we have an admin user type which presumably

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:49.845
means that it's just an admin that's also a user.

00:27:49.845 --> 00:27:54.550
And it'll- Rocket will run these for you automatically and it will only execute your function.

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:57.140
Your handler if these validations hold true.

00:27:57.140 --> 00:27:58.875
Okay. So, yeah.

00:27:58.875 --> 00:27:59.940
So, these Request Guards,

00:27:59.940 --> 00:28:02.550
they validate the incoming request and they protect handlers

00:28:02.550 --> 00:28:05.410
from running under, you know, erroneous conditions.

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:09.270
The guards may fail I- if you don't have an admin logged in.

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:12.060
Then, the guard will fail and

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:15.090
the request won't be processed any further because that indicates that there's,

00:28:15.090 --> 00:28:16.935
you know, something wrong with incoming request.

00:28:16.935 --> 00:28:20.550
The guard may also forward which indicates that it has failed,

00:28:20.550 --> 00:28:21.965
sort of, for now.

00:28:21.965 --> 00:28:25.865
But, something else might be able to- to process or- this request in the future.

00:28:25.865 --> 00:28:27.915
And I'll make this a little clearer in just a second.

00:28:27.915 --> 00:28:29.990
So, let's say we have, sort of,

00:28:29.990 --> 00:28:32.655
another- uh, another route here.

00:28:32.655 --> 00:28:35.695
So, you know that they both match get/admin.

00:28:35.695 --> 00:28:40.750
So, it's sort of there's no ordering with just get/admin so the given ordering,

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:43.865
you have to- you have to- you call these colliding routes.

00:28:43.865 --> 00:28:45.800
To give an ordering, you have to give them a rank,

00:28:45.800 --> 00:28:49.235
and if you don't give them a rank so- so you just had this.

00:28:49.235 --> 00:28:52.480
Then, um, so you can give a rank to sort of fix it.

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:54.465
If you don't give it a rank, if you have just this,

00:28:54.465 --> 00:28:58.190
then Rocket will sort of complain and tell you that there are colliding routes.

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:02.375
And it has to do this at runtime because routes can be generated, uh, dynamically.

00:29:02.375 --> 00:29:03.680
So unfortunately, we can't do it at compile time,

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:04.805
we have to do it at runtime.

00:29:04.805 --> 00:29:07.520
But, before your application does anything, all of this stuff is checked.

00:29:07.520 --> 00:29:10.365
So, this is again sort of trying to fix misconfiguration issues,

00:29:10.365 --> 00:29:13.675
and it will tell you exactly which routes are colliding and,

00:29:13.675 --> 00:29:17.310
you know, it'll give you a hint about how to fix it, particularly using ranking.

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:19.355
Okay. So, if we go back and rank these.

00:29:19.355 --> 00:29:23.070
Uh, Rocket will try colliding routes in ascending rank order.

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:25.090
So, first it'll try the route at the top,

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:26.585
and then it'll try the next route.

00:29:26.585 --> 00:29:28.190
So what's gonna happen here,

00:29:28.190 --> 00:29:32.290
is if someone makes a request to the /admin path, a get request.

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:37.375
Then that first route will run which requires their- there to be an admin user logged in.

00:29:37.375 --> 00:29:41.415
So that's going to do something like check the cookies for a session ID you know,

00:29:41.415 --> 00:29:43.685
check the database for that session,

00:29:43.685 --> 00:29:45.320
check that- that identifies a user,

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:47.880
check the- the role for that user is an admin and if so sort of

00:29:47.880 --> 00:29:51.555
deserialize the stuff in a database into this admin user type and finally return it.

00:29:51.555 --> 00:29:53.365
So, do all- all the things that needs to do.

00:29:53.365 --> 00:29:55.800
If that forwards, that means,

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:58.910
try the next thing, try the next route in the chain.

00:29:58.910 --> 00:30:00.790
If it- if it fails,

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:02.270
then it won't try anything else.

00:30:02.270 --> 00:30:03.540
So in this case, if it forwards,

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:04.900
it'll try that route at the bottom.

00:30:04.900 --> 00:30:09.180
This one also has sort of a requirement that there is a user logged in on an admin user,

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:11.215
but sort of just a regular user.

00:30:11.215 --> 00:30:13.060
And, uh, so in this case,

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:14.535
if there's a regular user logged in,

00:30:14.535 --> 00:30:15.870
it'll execute that second route,

00:30:15.870 --> 00:30:16.930
and if it can't,

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:18.600
if it forwards, it'll try the next one.

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:19.865
If there is no next one,

00:30:19.865 --> 00:30:21.040
it'll 404.

00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:22.670
If it fails, it'll sort of, uh,

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:26.420
404 immediately or 400 depending on how it's sort of implemented.

00:30:26.420 --> 00:30:28.310
And you can sort of extend this further,

00:30:28.310 --> 00:30:29.675
so we have a third route.

00:30:29.675 --> 00:30:32.385
If this user type forwards,

00:30:32.385 --> 00:30:33.950
then it'll go to this third route,

00:30:33.950 --> 00:30:35.000
this rank three route.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:37.740
In this case, there are no requirements for the request.

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:40.180
There's no- there are no types in the input which means

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:42.935
that you don't need any sort of validation, this will always run.

00:30:42.935 --> 00:30:45.210
So, if you imagine this sort of a real application,

00:30:45.210 --> 00:30:48.430
this is sort of implementing, authorization, and authentication.

00:30:48.430 --> 00:30:52.235
If there is an admin user presumably given the admin panel, the first thing runs.

00:30:52.235 --> 00:30:53.585
If there is a regular user,

00:30:53.585 --> 00:30:55.250
you sort of give him, you know, you tell him,

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:58.470
"Hey, you need to be an administrator to actually access the admin panel."

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:00.430
And if there is no one logged in,

00:31:00.430 --> 00:31:02.870
then you redirect them to the log-in page.

00:31:02.870 --> 00:31:05.095
So, this is sort of implementing authorization.

00:31:05.095 --> 00:31:08.380
Well, not sort of this is implementing, authorization, and authentication,

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:10.600
and sort of the policy for what it means to have

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:13.390
certain roles for an admin panel in one go.

00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:15.190
And Rocket is kind of doing everything for you.

00:31:15.190 --> 00:31:16.830
You just sort of write the happy case.

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:17.950
If there is an admin, do this.

00:31:17.950 --> 00:31:20.095
If there aren't an admin, but there's a user, do this.

00:31:20.095 --> 00:31:22.720
If there is nothing, then do this other thing.

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:26.560
Okay. So, that's sort of a combination of request guards and forwarding,

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:29.090
which I think is sort of the most interesting part here.

00:31:29.090 --> 00:31:32.320
Now, the last sort of feature I wanna talk about is Typed URIs,

00:31:32.320 --> 00:31:34.330
and this is sort of looking into how Rocket

00:31:34.330 --> 00:31:37.240
tries to try to help you in terms of correctness.

00:31:37.240 --> 00:31:39.465
So, let's say you have a route that looks like this.

00:31:39.465 --> 00:31:41.585
You're implementing some kind of shopping cart or something,

00:31:41.585 --> 00:31:46.895
and you allow people to get an item of a certain kind so like a shirt or something,

00:31:46.895 --> 00:31:49.695
and of a certain id like 23 or something.

00:31:49.695 --> 00:31:51.320
So, you're gonna run this, you know,

00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:52.860
someone will request this product with that id,

00:31:52.860 --> 00:31:54.705
you're gonna return them the item,

00:31:54.705 --> 00:31:56.750
and that's gonna render it somehow.

00:31:56.750 --> 00:32:00.025
So, if you want to get URL to a particular product,

00:32:00.025 --> 00:32:03.250
well, you need to have a valid product name and a valid id.

00:32:03.250 --> 00:32:06.130
So, one thing you could do is you can just, you know,

00:32:06.130 --> 00:32:09.140
create this random string so you can sort of, uh,

00:32:09.140 --> 00:32:10.775
do string interpolation with, uh,

00:32:10.775 --> 00:32:14.295
the word shirt and with the number of 2417 and get a string.

00:32:14.295 --> 00:32:19.115
But shirt may no longer be your product like so you stop using shirt as a product or,

00:32:19.115 --> 00:32:21.235
you know, this URL just changed completely.

00:32:21.235 --> 00:32:23.760
Uh, instead of inventory, you had it somewhere else.

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:27.335
And who knows if 2417 is a valid id?

00:32:27.335 --> 00:32:30.250
So, sort of the problem here is we wanna create these URIs,

00:32:30.250 --> 00:32:32.200
but we also wanna make sure these URIs are correct.

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:33.570
And to do that,

00:32:33.570 --> 00:32:35.590
we're gonna use the type information when you use the fact

00:32:35.590 --> 00:32:38.340
that sh- product has to be of the type product,

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:41.680
an id has to be a usize to sort of create valid ids.

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:43.215
So, instead of doing this format thing,

00:32:43.215 --> 00:32:44.525
we're gonna do this URI thing.

00:32:44.525 --> 00:32:48.255
We're gonna say URI bang for the route named item,

00:32:48.255 --> 00:32:49.960
parsed in the type parameters,

00:32:49.960 --> 00:32:52.770
product shirt, and the number 2417.

00:32:52.770 --> 00:32:54.630
And what Rocket is gonna do is gonna,

00:32:54.630 --> 00:32:57.160
you know, check these things against the actual route.

00:32:57.160 --> 00:32:58.975
So, sort of if, if you,

00:32:58.975 --> 00:33:00.850
if you wanna pass in th- the names,

00:33:00.850 --> 00:33:02.470
you can do that, you can flip them around.

00:33:02.470 --> 00:33:05.540
But if you get them wrong, so if you do item equals product shirt.

00:33:05.540 --> 00:33:08.860
And you can see that there's no item parameter in the URL,

00:33:08.860 --> 00:33:10.150
then Rocket will complain.

00:33:10.150 --> 00:33:13.005
It will say, "You tried to pass in a parameter named item,

00:33:13.005 --> 00:33:15.085
but there's no such parameter in the route."

00:33:15.085 --> 00:33:17.100
And so this thing will just fail at compile time.

00:33:17.100 --> 00:33:19.765
If you get the wrong type, you're gonna get the same issue.

00:33:19.765 --> 00:33:23.180
You're gonna get not the same error, but a similar error.

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:25.400
And, uh, finally, if everything sort of works out okay,

00:33:25.400 --> 00:33:26.600
you're gonna get this, uh,

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:29.305
you're gonna get this string at compile time.

00:33:29.305 --> 00:33:31.235
So all of this is sort of happening at compile time,

00:33:31.235 --> 00:33:32.840
the type checking is happening at compile time,

00:33:32.840 --> 00:33:35.990
the name of URL parameter checking is happening at compile time.

00:33:35.990 --> 00:33:37.850
So you really can't get URIs wrong.

00:33:37.850 --> 00:33:39.230
If you change, you know,

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:41.305
this route to have a different URI later,

00:33:41.305 --> 00:33:43.800
this, this thing is guaranteed to fail to compile,

00:33:43.800 --> 00:33:46.070
if it's changed in a type-incompatible way.

00:33:46.070 --> 00:33:47.900
So, it's just the sort of a small way,

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:50.360
but a very helpful way that Rocket tries to,

00:33:50.360 --> 00:33:53.165
you know, fix correctness issues in applications.

00:33:53.165 --> 00:33:57.200
Okay, so that's most of what I want to say about, about Rocket.

00:33:57.200 --> 00:33:59.590
Now, there is one last thing that I sort of mentioned

00:33:59.590 --> 00:34:02.355
in this introduction thing which was performance.

00:34:02.355 --> 00:34:05.340
So, the last thing I want to talk about is sort of how

00:34:05.340 --> 00:34:08.725
Rocket does in comparison to other web frameworks.

00:34:08.725 --> 00:34:12.560
And, uh, so I compared against sort of what people are using.

00:34:12.560 --> 00:34:13.940
Nowadays, there's no Java,

00:34:13.940 --> 00:34:16.420
which presumably is what people are using in Enterprise.

00:34:16.420 --> 00:34:18.280
But it's, when I compare against Java my guess is,

00:34:18.280 --> 00:34:20.210
it's somewhere between what I did compare it against.

00:34:20.210 --> 00:34:24.740
What I did compare it against are these sort of free web frameworks.

00:34:24.740 --> 00:34:27.600
Rails, which is the Ruby web framework, Express,

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:28.825
which is the Node web framework,

00:34:28.825 --> 00:34:30.820
and Flask, which is a Python web framework.

00:34:30.820 --> 00:34:34.540
Sort of a minimalistic Python web framework, and then Rocket.

00:34:34.540 --> 00:34:37.110
So, this is a measure of throughput.

00:34:37.110 --> 00:34:41.000
This is on a sort of 12 core eight, nine-year-old machine.

00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:43.380
So, presumably if you run this on your own servers,

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:44.895
you're gonna get, you know, four, five,

00:34:44.895 --> 00:34:46.170
6x what we see here.

00:34:46.170 --> 00:34:47.675
Uh, yeah.

00:34:47.675 --> 00:34:48.760
So, this is throughput.

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:52.535
So, Rocket gets around 38,000 requests per second here.

00:34:52.535 --> 00:34:54.150
Express is the next leader,

00:34:54.150 --> 00:34:55.950
this is Node, this is Javascript.

00:34:55.950 --> 00:34:58.740
This is around 20, 27 or so.

00:34:58.740 --> 00:35:02.085
So Rocket gets around 12,000 more requests per second,

00:35:02.085 --> 00:35:03.930
and Express ended, you know,

00:35:03.930 --> 00:35:05.490
both of them do significantly better,

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:06.595
better than what you get in,

00:35:06.595 --> 00:35:07.970
in either Rails or Flask.

00:35:07.970 --> 00:35:12.080
So, this is okay. Really, we really see really big differences, and this is a lot,

00:35:12.080 --> 00:35:14.515
there's a lot to do with the language that it's written in,

00:35:14.515 --> 00:35:16.650
is when we look at latency.

00:35:16.650 --> 00:35:18.070
So these are the numbers for latency.

00:35:18.070 --> 00:35:19.620
This is average latency.

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:22.190
So, Rails, Express, and Flask,

00:35:22.190 --> 00:35:23.375
all have sort of,

00:35:23.375 --> 00:35:24.755
this is on local network by the way.

00:35:24.755 --> 00:35:27.585
Obviously, you're not gonna see sub-microsecond latency on, on a sort of, uh,

00:35:27.585 --> 00:35:29.365
on the Internet, that's, that'll be awesome,

