00:47:14.600 --> 00:47:15.840
you can do it for whatever.

00:47:15.840 --> 00:47:17.480
You can do for your own,

00:47:17.480 --> 00:47:19.085
you know, networking protocols.

00:47:19.085 --> 00:47:21.030
Um, yeah so and we've even,

00:47:21.030 --> 00:47:25.060
we've even extended this further to like system calls which kind of look like RPCs.

00:47:25.060 --> 00:47:26.665
Anything that kind of looks like RPC,

00:47:26.665 --> 00:47:28.100
this, this fits in perfectly.

00:47:28.100 --> 00:47:29.850
So, we've done it for like system calls,

00:47:29.850 --> 00:47:31.590
where someone calls you and you need to validate

00:47:31.590 --> 00:47:33.495
all the inputs before you actually do any processing.

00:47:33.495 --> 00:47:35.400
So, yeah, it's kind of a general mechanism,

00:47:35.400 --> 00:47:36.795
but it's really well suited for HTTP.

00:47:36.795 --> 00:47:37.000
Great.

